# 🧵 Secure Coding Series #3: Output Encoding – Escape Before You Display

## 📌 What is Output Encoding?

**Output encoding** means converting user-provided data into a safe format **before displaying it** on a webpage, UI, or any output channel.

The goal is to prevent **malicious content** (like scripts or HTML) from being interpreted as **code**, instead forcing it to be shown as **plain text**.

It is a key defense against:

- Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)
- HTML/JS injection
- Broken UI rendering

---

## ⚠️ What Happens Without Output Encoding?

Let’s say a user enters this comment:
(html)
<script>alert("Hacked!")</script>

If you display it without encoding:

<div>User says: <script>alert("Hacked!")</script></div>

The browser executes the script → You’ve been XSS’d 💥

If you encode the input before displaying:

<div>User says: &lt;script&gt;alert("Hacked!")&lt;/script&gt;</div>

The browser will now render it as text, not code:

User says: <script>alert("Hacked!")</script>

No alert, no execution, no hack ✅

🔴 Insecure: Python Flask (Jinja2 disabled auto-escaping)

from flask import Flask, render_template_string, request

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/')
def index():
    comment = request.args.get("comment", "")
    # Autoescaping is disabled — this is dangerous
    return render_template_string("<p>User says: %s</p>" % comment)

🧨 ?comment=<script>alert('XSS')</script> -> XSS


✅ Secure: Python Flask (with escaping)

from flask import Flask, render_template_string, request
import html

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/')
def index():
    comment = request.args.get("comment", "")
    # Manual backend encoding
    safe_comment = html.escape(comment)
    return render_template_string("<p>User says: %s</p>" % safe_comment)

output:
<p>User says: &lt;script&gt;alert('XSS')&lt;/script&gt;</p>

(C#)
public ActionResult Index(string comment)
{
    ViewBag.Comment = comment;
    return View();
}


